 "feat_d_logfpr": "-0.36 (95% CI -0.50 to -0.18)",
 "dom_auc_rng": "1.000",
 "dom_auc_rob_rng": "0.977 to 1.000",
 "feat_fpr_all": "13.7",
 "feat_fpr_rob": "7.5",
 "feat_sentence": "The tool-stable features thus lower the mean false-alarm inflation from 13.7 to 7.5 times nominal but do not restore the ranking, and benign traffic stays almost perfectly separable, which points to differences between the networks, rather than the extractor, as the main cause of the ranking loss.",
 "feat_disc": "Restricting the detector to features computed identically by all CICFlowMeter versions reduced the false-alarm flood but did not restore the ranking (Section 4.7).",
 "feat_lim": "Restricting the features to those computed identically across CICFlowMeter versions reduced the false-alarm inflation but left AUROC and the separability of benign traffic almost unchanged, which bounds the share of tooling shift without removing it",
 "resp_feat": "With all features, the benign flows of two datasets are separable with an AUROC of 1.000, and with the tool-stable features 0.977 to 1.000. Restricting the detector to these features changes cross-dataset AUROC by +0.025 on average (95% CI -0.048 to +0.126, six pairs) and lowers the mean false-alarm inflation from 13.7 to 7.5 times nominal. Tooling therefore explains part of the false-alarm flood but not the loss of ranking, which we attribute mainly to differences between the networks; Section 5.4 states that public data do not allow a complete separation.",
 "og_n_groups": "15",
 "og_n_below_half": "9",
 "og_median_recall": "0.162",
 "ref_auc_20k": "0.806",
 "ref_auc_1k": "0.819",
 "ref_d_1k": "-0.045 (95% CI -0.078 to -0.007; p < 0.001, 39 families)",
 "ref_d_50k": "+0.019 (95% CI +0.009 to +0.029; p < 0.001, 31 families)",
 "ref_d_c1": "-0.172 (95% CI -0.235 to -0.117; p < 0.001, 39 families)",
 "ref_d_c5": "-0.220 (95% CI -0.303 to -0.145; p < 0.001, 29 families)",
 "ref_d_c01": "-0.099 (95% CI -0.150 to -0.056; p < 0.001, 39 families)",
 "ref_c1_pct": "1%",
 "sub_lo_load": "1.01",
 "sub_hi_load": "1.25",
 "sub_sentence": "In every dataset and protocol, the order of the detector and the Fisher priority at a 1% budget is the same at all three benign fractions, and the warm-up load stays between 1.01 and 1.25 times the budget. Cross-dataset AUROC of the CIC-IDS2017 models stays between 0.687 and 0.714, and Fisher recall on held-out CIC-IDS2017 families between 0.630 and 0.647. The one clear effect is on ToN-IoT, where Fisher recall on held-out families falls from 0.679 with all benign training flows to 0.533 with a quarter of them",
 "resp_sub": "The ranking of the triage rules and the budget behaviour of the warm-up quantile were the same at all three fractions (Section 4.8, Appendix Table A12). Recall on held-out ToN-IoT families fell when we removed three quarters of the benign training flows, so the absolute recall values for the subsampled copies are, if anything, conservative; we state this in Section 4.8.",
 "resp_ref": "Reducing the benign reference from 20,000 to 1,000 flows changes Fisher recall at a 1% budget by -0.045, and increasing it to 50,000 flows by +0.019. Contamination matters far more: with 0.1% of the reference drawn from the held-out family, recall changes by -0.099, and with 1% by -0.172 (Appendix Table A13). Exact kNN cost grows linearly with the reference, and an approximate index removes most of that growth (Table 8).",
 "og_d_fisher": "+0.075 (95% CI +0.019 to +0.128; p = 0.005, 15 groups)",
